From: Pyro project
Subject: [PATCH] fs/bfs: Don't free an uninitialized node when a lookup fails

find_file() passes &found to grub_fshelp_find_file_lookup(), which only
sets it on success. On any failure (a missing file, a dangling symlink, a
bad path component) found is left uninitialized, and the function then
frees it unless it happens to equal &root. Depending on what is on the
stack this crashes ("double free or corruption" from grub-fstest 2.06,
SIGSEGV from a 2.12 build) or silently corrupts the heap. It affects both
the bfs and afs modules.

Initialize found to NULL and free it only if it was set.

Reproduced with grub-fstest on Syllable AFS images: "cp (loop0,msdos1)/nope"
or reading through a dangling symlink crashes before the fix and reports
"file not found" after it.

---
--- a/grub-core/fs/bfs.c
+++ b/grub-core/fs/bfs.c
@@ -809,7 +809,7 @@
     .disk = disk,
     .sb = sb,
   };
-  struct grub_fshelp_node *found;
+  struct grub_fshelp_node *found = NULL;
 
   err = read_extent (disk, sb, &sb->root_dir, 0, 0, &root.ino,
 		     sizeof (root.ino));
@@ -819,7 +819,7 @@
   if (!err)
     grub_memcpy (ino, &found->ino, sizeof (*ino));
 
-  if (&root != found)
+  if (found != NULL && &root != found)
     grub_free (found);
   return err;
 }
